      <!-- Stat strip. Grid lines are the section's background showing through 1px gaps —
           no per-cell border logic, so a divider can't go missing at any breakpoint
           (the old per-cell borders lost the mobile row lines and the md col-3 line).
           The last stat spans the remainder of its row so no empty cell is left. -->
      <section class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-px border-x border-b border-rule bg-rule md:grid-cols-3 lg:grid-cols-5">
        {stats.map((s, i) => (
          <div class={`bg-paper px-4 py-4 ${i === stats.length - 1 ? "col-span-2 lg:col-span-1" : ""}`}>
            <p class="text-xs text-faint">{s.label}</p>
            <p class="tabular mt-1 font-mono text-xl font-semibold text-ink">
              {s.value}
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-[11px] leading-snug text-faint">{s.sub}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </section>

      <!-- Brent YTD + depletion-runway quadrants -->
      <section id="prices" class="pb-12 pt-20">
        <div class="mb-10">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Prices</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-ink/70">
            These charts show how oil, gasoline, diesel, and natural gas prices have changed.
            Brent is a widely used benchmark for the price of crude oil. The gasoline and diesel
            charts use US national averages; the natural gas charts cover Europe and Asia.
            The captions explain where the readings come from. We leave gaps where data is missing.
          </p>
        </div>
      <!-- US fuels YTD -->
      <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div>
          <div class="mb-4 flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-2">
            <h2 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight">US gasoline prices are up 59%</h2>
            <p class="font-mono text-[11px] text-faint">EIA weekly and AAA daily national averages</p>
          </div>
          <div class="h-72">
            <canvas id="gasChart" role="img" aria-label="US gasoline, EIA weekly national average (AAA daily readings Sep 10 to 23): $2.81 in January, May peak $4.50, $4.47 on Sep 23. Reference line at the January pre-crisis $2.81."></canvas>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-3 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-ink/70">
            The national average has risen from $2.81 in January to $4.47, an increase of about 59%.
            Prices peaked at $4.50 in May. Every month since March has averaged above the levels
            seen before the crisis.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="mb-4 flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-2">
            <h2 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight">US diesel prices are up 75%</h2>
            <p class="font-mono text-[11px] text-faint">AAA national average · dots on selected dates</p>
          </div>
          <div class="h-72">
            <canvas id="dieselChart" role="img" aria-label="US diesel, AAA national: $3.52 in January, breaking the June 2022 record of about $5.85 on Sep 4, passing $6.00 for the first time on Sep 11 — all-time record $6.53 on Sep 22, easing to $6.52 on Sep 23."></canvas>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-3 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-ink/70">
            Diesel cost $3.72 on Feb 27, just before the war. It reached a record $6.53 on
            Sep 22, then eased to $6.52 on Sep 23. That's still about 75% above its pre-war price.
            It first passed the previous record of about $5.85, set in June 2022, on Sep 4.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Natural gas: TTF (Europe) + JKM (Asia) — verified series, two sources, no interpolation -->
      <div class="mt-12">
        <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2">
          <div>
            <div class="mb-4">
              <h2 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight">Natural gas prices in Europe are up about 120%</h2>
              <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-[11px] text-faint">TTF, dollars per million British thermal units ($/MMBtu). Filled dots: EIA weekly futures averages (Jan–Apr). Hollow dots: individual contract prices and Trading Economics quotes (May–Sep). Contracts and delivery periods differ, so comparisons are approximate.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="h-72">
              <canvas id="gasTtfChart" role="img" aria-label="TTF, Europe's gas benchmark, 2026 year to date: $12.40 in January, $11.00 before the closure, spiking to a three-year high of $18.89 on Mar 20, easing to $14.10 after the June ceasefire, $19.90 on Jul 31, then trading above $28 on Sep 10, its highest level since December 2022, and $27.00 on Sep 11. Market quotes: about $27.80 on Sep 14, $26.70 on Sep 18, and $24.20 on Sep 22. Reference line at the pre-closure week's $10.99."></canvas>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-ink/70">
              TTF is the benchmark used to track natural gas prices in Europe. It traded above
              $28 on Sep 10, its highest level since December 2022, before easing to $27.00 on
              Sep 11. Prices have since eased: the latest quote was about $24.20 on Sep 22
              (€72.10 per megawatt-hour), still about 120% above the price before the strait
              closed. Its Sep 14, Sep 18, and Sep 22 quotes (€81.98, €79.52, and €72.10 per
              megawatt-hour) are converted to the dollar units used here. They come from market
              quotes, including contracts for difference (CFDs), financial contracts that track gas prices.
              Unplanned maintenance at Norway's Asgard and Troll fields and tanker attacks have
              reduced supplies as prices have risen.
              </p>
              <p class="mt-3 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-ink/70">
              By January 20, European gas storage had fallen to 48%, compared with a five-year
              average of 63%. With less gas in storage, Europe was buying liquefied natural gas (LNG)
              for immediate delivery during the season when the strait closed.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="mb-4">
              <h2 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight">Natural gas prices in Asia are up about 158%</h2>
